why 2 0 SIMILAR_MOTION A cadence in A minor where every voice falls: soprano B–A, the alto's G♯ drops to E, the seventh D to C and the bass to A.
why 6 1/2 LEAPS The bass climbs B–C–E–F, mostly through chord notes. The tritone B–F is spread over several notes and two chords.
why 9 1/8 SPACING The tune leaps up a sixth to E while the alto stays on D. The gap lasts an eighth, as the tune falls back D–C–B.
why 9 1/4 LEADING_TONE The tune leaps up from G♯ to E, and Bach keeps the hymn melody. The A that G♯ leads to arrives in the alto at the same moment.
why 9 1/4 OVERLAP Alto and tenor both leap up, D–A and B–E, as the tune leaps up. They move together, so the order stays clear.
why 9 3/4 SPACING The bass drops an octave to a low E for the final dominant while the tenor stays in its middle range. The deep bass gives the cadence weight.
why 9 7/8 SPACING The same wide gap, as the tenor adds the seventh D before the last chord.
